 "overview": "Senator Jeff Merkley seeks a fourth term in a state that has favored Democrats by about eight points at the presidential level. His Republican opponent, state Senator David Brock Smith, won a contested primary but enters the general election with minimal resources, having raised roughly $121,000 to Merkley's $4.5 million, with the incumbent holding nearly $6.8 million on hand. No outside groups have invested in the race on either side.\n\nThere are no general-election polls, but every major expert rating places the race in the Safe or Solid Democratic column, and prediction markets give Merkley a 92 to 98 percent chance of winning. The forecast expects a comfortable Merkley victory, with uncertainty driven mainly by the absence of polling rather than any sign of a competitive race.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9939, "margin": 17.74}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9912, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.994, "margin": 17.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9889, "margin": 17.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9925, "margin": 17.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9912, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9935, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9929, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9932, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9932, "margin": 18.0}]}